# Rotation for the usage log.
#
#   sudo install -m0644 deploy/hcr-usage.logrotate /etc/logrotate.d/hcr-usage
#
# The service has no rotation of its own on purpose: every row carries an epoch
# `ts`, so bucketing by day is a property of the data rather than of the
# filename, and logrotate is already on the box and better at this.
#
# Rotation renames the live file and creates nothing in its place. hcr-server
# compares its descriptor against the path before every write, and when rotation
# has moved the file it reopens the path, creating the file 0640 under the
# unit's `UMask=0027`. `follows_the_path_when_rotation_renames_the_file` in
# crates/hcr/src/usage.rs pins that. A build older than that check keeps
# appending to the renamed file, so install this only alongside a current build.
#
# Not `copytruncate`: it keeps the original inode, so the file never gets a
# tighter mode, and rows written between the copy and the truncate are lost.
# `nocreate` overrides a global `create` in /etc/logrotate.conf, which would race
# the server's own reopen. `su hcr hcr` rotates as the service user, so root never
# renames or compresses files in a directory that hcr can write to.

/var/lib/hcr/usage.jsonl {
	su hcr hcr
	daily
	rotate 90
	compress
	delaycompress
	missingok
	notifempty
	nocreate
}
